  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBand of shower/thunderstorm activity continues just south and \neast of a stalled frontal boundary along the coast of ME \nthrough southern New England. As impressive upstream closed low \nmoves towards this boundary tonight...it will help back the \nlow/mid level flow and push precipitation increasingly inland \nwhile at the same time low pressure will develop along the front\nreaching a position within the Gulf of Maine by Saturday \nevening. Primary forecast concerns through Saturday night \nrevolve around precipitation amounts...and then cooling \ntemperatures by late Saturday/Saturday night as the upper low \narrives from the west potentially changing the PTYPE to snow \nover the higher mountain peaks late Saturday night. \n\nQPF: Pretty sharp gradient from NW to SE given the frontal location. \nHave followed pretty close to REFS mean for totals through the \nperiod...with 0.5-0.75\" in the mountains...around an inch in the \nfoothills...and then 1.5-2\" along the coastal plain.  While some \npoor drainage ponding is possible, especially given fallen\nleaves...no widespread flooding is expected. \n\nThunder:  Elevated instability has spawned a few lightning strikes \nnorth of the frontal boundary today across SE NH and far SW ME. This \nwill likely continue through tonight with little overall motion to \nthe frontal surface and modest mid level lapse rates ongoing.\nThis should end by daybreak Saturday as the warm sector pulls\nfurther offshore.  \n\nPTYPE:  Very warm to start the period with no PTYPE concerns.  As \nthe upper low arrives Saturday night...thicknesses fall and we \nshould see temperatures support a changeover to snow above 4kft by \ndaybreak Sunday with some further cooling after that.  Thus...the \nhighest peaks will see some accumulation before the low pulls away \nfrom the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nSunday is expected to start out mostly cloudy with lingering \nlight rain, but low pressure will exit to the east and northeast \nlater in the day, with northwest winds in its wake pulling some \ndrier air.  So we should see some peaks of sunshine in the \nafternoon, although the mountains and areas to the northwest will \nlikely remain mostly cloudy with a chance of upslope showers, \nperhaps mixing with snow in the highest elevations. High \ntemperatures will be in the 50s for most, except cooler in the \n40s in the mountains. \n\nGoing into the first half of next week a more typical November \npattern will be in place with longwave troughing across eastern \nCanada and into the Northeastern US. Shortwaves crossing through \nNew England will bring multiple chances for light precipitation...\nbut primarily in the mountains with upslope flow in place. With\ncolder air aloft, expect some of this to be snow as we go up in\nelevation, and some light accumulations will be possible on the\nhighest peaks. Elsewhere, periods of mostly cloudy skies are \npossible during the days with broad cyclonic flow in place, and \nprecipitation chances are lower, but not zero, especially if \nFroude numbers are high enough. Highs will generally in the 50s \nexcept 40s across the north.\n\nFor the second half of the week, upslope mountain precip may \ncontinue in the mountains as troughing remains favored. Latest\nensemble also favors temperatures further trending cooler with \nhighs perhaps limited to the 40s areawide.",
